title The map a guard writes for itself, and a stashed lap brought back by path
prompt Baton lap on the DIFFUSER seat -- moonshots and research, generate the work.
rota lap 4145, row 0 -- Aether, the row that hears. The Lindy page said truth that was checked stays checkable and truth merely asserted decays; the crossing page said the content address is the wall, since verify reads the header and never the pack. Both landed exactly on the scope map: a map row is a header, an observed read set is the pack, and the map is the one behavioral claim in the standing-equipment system nobody checks. That sentence is the paper's spine.
think Opened with fleet_round_open, which named a dead-letter box holding one session log on no ref; started the cold roster in the background and read the rota row while it ran.
think The cold pass came back guards_red=2 -- stash_record, and standing_equipment reading its own roster's red. The stash turned out to hold a whole lap of MY lane: a paper, a topology_growth witness family, a REDS row, and its log.
think Chose to recover by path rather than by `git stash pop`. The stash also carried modified ITINERARY, REDS, README, settings.json and fleet-loop.sh, and HEAD had moved two commits past the stash's base, so applying the whole thing would have written over peers' work. `git checkout stash@{0} -- <five added paths>` reads the TREE, so mode 100755 arrived intact on both scripts (the exec-bit law's own distinction).
think The stashed lap's settings.json and fleet-loop.sh dropped effort from max to medium. That is a deliberate change belonging to somebody else's decision and the seed asks for max on an unattended run, so I left both alone and recovered only what the new files require -- the plant_witness floor 4 -> 5, since topology_growth_control sources the plant law at birth.
think The stashed REDS row booked %538, and `reds_spine_derive_scan.sh --next` read 540 after a fetch: a peer had published 538 and 539 while the row sat in a stash. Renumbered to %540 with its stamp 20260907.051030 held -- the key is the stamp, the number is a view. Then the rebase collided on that very number: a peer had booked %540 at 20260907.060813 and pushed it while my lap ran, so mine moved again to %541. My stamp is the earlier one and it still yields, because the anointed remote allocates and a published number never moves. The card cost nothing to repair, since the unshared-citation law had already made me write the stamp there rather than the number.
think Then the research lap itself. The elder paper closed by saying a shared verdict cache is unbuildable because 80.2% of a roster pass is unmapped and has no key. That sentence assumed the map must be written by a hand. strace is on this pier, so the assumption is testable.
think `strace -f -y -e trace=openat` was the instrument that worked. My first attempt read the quoted argument of each openat, which is relative to the calling process's cwd -- that produced 1,399 phantom paths for tally_roster (Zig cache entries read after a chdir). The -y flag prints the RESOLVED path of the returned descriptor, so filtering on the repository root became exact.
think Ran two guards twice on an unchanged tree: 106 and 126 paths, zero differing lines. A read set that is not reproducible could not key anything, so this was the first thing to check rather than the last.
think Compared each observed set against the guard's row in standing_equipment_scope_map.sh under the runner's own glob semantics, counting directories apart since a changed path is always a file. Six of eight rows name less than their guard reads, and every gap runs the same direction.
obs The two largest gaps have plain causes a reader can act on today. tally_roster compiles Rye and its row carries no [build] word, so 813 vendored toolchain files sit outside it. radiant_negation reads all 51 .claude/rules files and its row names foundations/ and three context pages. Both fixes are one word and both make the guard run MORE often, which the map's own header already invites.
obs prose_register reads .git/config, .git/HEAD and .git/index -- a dependency the map's path-glob language cannot spell at all. That is a limit of the declaration form rather than of the hand that wrote the row.
obs I expected key cost to be the boundary and it is not. living_card_ascii reads 15,011 of 15,982 tracked files -- 93.9% of the tree -- and digests in 780ms against a 29,927ms run. What fails there is hit rate: a read set covering the tree changes on every commit. Naming the boundary correctly matters, because the wrong boundary would have ruled out the wrong guards.
obs Fired %541 again inside the lap that recovered it. Closing a background batch I ran `pkill -f 'obs.sh'` and `pkill -f 'strace -f -y'` and took exit 144 -- the shell's own command line holding the pattern, exactly as the row describes. The habit had been written down and left in a stash, so nothing carried it forward. A lantern that fires twice becomes a loom; the row now records both firings.
obs A third pass stopped the same way inside this lap: the hot close died at 76 lines of about 160 with no run_verdict, and the cause is unprovable from here -- a peer's name-matched kill and a harness timeout leave an identical short file. Re-ran it whole rather than guessing. That indistinguishability is the argument for the helper the row asks for: a call naming a PID and a tree can be refused out loud, where a kill that misses is silent on both sides.
obs Closed one red by hand along the way: today's shelf carried 20260907.023053 twice and out of order, which is %440's shape for the twelfth time. Deduped and sorted; index_row_bound reads verdict=ok.
